  a person's reputation is so important  that they are justified in doing  almost anything to protect it up to and  including  dropping a bomb on a critic's house in  this episode slappy and skippy squirrel  tortures siskel and ebert stand-ins  hisskel and egbert until they publicly  retract a negative review  now here's today's lesson skippy if you  want to go on national tv and shred  someone's career to pieces you have the  right  right but when you do that remember  don't go listing your home address in  the phone book while these jokes made at  hollywood's expense are mostly pretty  light-hearted the show can sometimes get  downright  cynical in its approach to the industry  this becomes particularly clear in its  commentary on the economics of  entertainment michael  the team talks with 20th fox means he  wants another 93 million  and how our favorite movies and tv shows  are inextricably tied up with the flow  of money and power  animaniacs talks about this a lot in a  show that's supposedly written for  kids there's a near constant discussion  of legal documents music licensing and  other industry topics children wouldn't  know the first thing about  contracts for instance are more than a  passing mention in the show's opening  theme song  in both the original series  and the 2020 reboot you should see our  new contract  the topic of contract negotiations also  plays a recurring role throughout both  iterations of the series wait a second  we got a contract our agent  with management and talent arguing  viciously about who should get paid how  much for what kind of work  the agent these kids got for themselves  was tough a real grinder  in one of the warner's early adventures  to the middle ages side characters  lament the fact that they can't sing  songs from camelot without paying for  the rights  [Music]  this preoccupation with money and  entertainment carries over into the 2020  reboot  the first episode of which ends with the  warner's accepting a giant  check moments after singing a song that  trashes hollywood's lack of originality  and constant recycling of old ideas  again and again the show implies that  money not artistic or cultural merit  is the main driver of what gets made in  hollywood and while this take on the  industry might feel a little jaded  especially for a kid show at least one  scholar would agree with it  in his book communication and capitalism  sociologist christian fuchs argues that  within the context of our capitalist  society  the purpose of the entertainment  industry is to produce attention that is  your attention which can then be  packaged up and sold to advertisers and  other buyers  who will pay good money to monopolize  your eyes and ears for 30 seconds at a  time from this bleak point of view  the entertainment industry looks less  like a collection of artists  trying to make beautiful moving films  and more like an assembly line of  factory workers laboring to transform  your time into marketable attention and  as in many factories working conditions  are  less than ideal fuegs contends that the  communication workers of the world are  united by the fact that they  all produce for and are exploited by  global communications corporations  put another way nearly everyone in the  entertainment industry is being squeezed  by the person above them to produce as  much as possible and return for payment  that doesn't nearly cover the value of  what they've made and sacrificed  this dynamic pops up again and again  throughout animaniacs with directors and  executives  leaning on creatives to constantly come  up with more and better work  get to places even if it destroys them  in the process  the 65th anniversary special for  instance imagines the warner's original  director as an angry boss who belittles  his employees and forces them to produce  above and beyond what could be  reasonably expected stay up all night if  you have to  i want funny the pressure gets so bad  that the creator loses his mind while  designing the characters  [Laughter]  and ultimately winds up in a mental  hospital later i found out he went all  funny in the head but i hear he's  getting better  we're having soup today in other words  according to the show's own logic  the warners and by implication many of  our favorite cartoon icons are a direct  result  of the industry's exploitation of an  animator a worker who in this case was  driven to insanity by over exertion  with regards to worker exploitation  little has changed between the series  original  run and the reboot the new fictionalized  warner brothers studio ceo is just as  willing to exploit her workers  as were her predecessors i don't care  that your girlfriend's new nose fell  into a subway toilet  we need that rewrite by the end of the  day the reboot also uses the animaniacs  formula to offer funny new critiques of  more recent industry developments in  particular  it has some interesting things to say  about the way that production and  advertising have evolved since the show  originally aired  in brief it satirizes the ways in which  content development is increasingly  driven  not by the decisions of individual  artists and executives but by a reliance  on data collected from prospective  audience members  animated x seems to be suggesting that  in a weird slightly creepy way  the perspective audience of a given  piece of film has become an unknowing  participant in the creative process  driving decisions about what gets made  and how  our friend fugues would agree and goes  so far as to argue that audience members  themselves have become  unpaid workers in an overarching  economic structure that includes the  entertainment industry  the advertising marketing industry and  social media platforms  as he puts it audience members are  audience workers creating attention as a  commodity while  users of ad financed internet platforms  are digital workers creating attention  data metadata and social relations as a  commodity  to put that in the simplest possible  terms without necessarily signing up for  it you become a worker in the worldwide  process of attention production  and you're on the job anytime you engage  with content watching it liking it  sharing it  talking about it and so on if you're  like much of the internet connected  world  you may spend hours in a given week  consuming and evaluating videos music  podcasts etc  in effect producing highly valuable  information and content for which you  never see a dime  but that's a video for another day the  animate ex reboot  calls out this new metrics obsessed  media landscape  directly in its theme song a brand new  cast who tested well in focus group  what's more in a funny musical number in  which the warners try to predict what  will happen between the reboots 2018  production and its 2020 release  the siblings acknowledge that their show  will succeed or fail based on how  quickly they can make themselves  relevant to whatever's being talked  about at the moment  [Music]  [Applause]  but the reboot's most direct response to  the quirky slash  terrifying implications of new media  comes in a pinky and the brain sketch  of mice and memes in it we learned that  way back in the 90s  the brain created the got dang internet  specifically for the purpose of  brainwashing and controlling the entire  planet here in 2020  he quickly gathers that the most  effective way to use this global  communications tool  is to gather as much attention from  internet users as possible  with their undivided attention they are  the perfect  unsuspecting pawns for my master plan  but of course like any self-aware  meta-happy show animated x eventually  has to offer some assessment of  its own role in the massive complicated  system that is the global entertainment  industry  we know we're selling out so it's cool  at least they're honest  what do you think wisecrack is the  animaniac's take on the entertainment  industry too pess